# Step apps deliberately do NOT pin their dependencies.
#
# The codelab tells the learner to run `flutter pub add flutter_edge_ai …`, which
# resolves to the newest compatible release — so that is what these apps must
# resolve to as well. A committed lock file would also blind the nightly CI run,
# whose entire purpose is to notice when a new package release breaks a step.
#
# The cost, stated plainly: a PR run is not reproducible. A transitive package
# publishing a broken version at 09:00 fails an unrelated PR at 09:05, and the
# author cannot tell "my change broke it" from "the world moved". Dart's own
# guidance is that applications commit `pubspec.lock`, and these are
# applications — this repo trades that for rot detection on purpose. A
# maintainer's local run also reuses their own stale lock while CI resolves
# fresh, so local green and CI green are not quite the same claim.
pubspec.lock

# The same reasoning on the CocoaPods side. `pod install` writes a Podfile.lock
# carrying the pod versions and SPEC CHECKSUMS of whichever machine ran it —
# one did reach a commit here — and these apps must resolve to today's pods for
# the same reason they resolve to today's packages.
Podfile.lock

# Package skills a learner installs with `dart run skills@ get`. They belong to
# flutter_edge_ai's release, not to these apps: a committed copy would drift from
# the package the next time it ships, and the codelab teaches installing them.
.agents/
.claude/
.config/dart_skills/
